WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.926
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.926 --> 00:00:14.926
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:19.294 --> 00:00:20.624
&gt;&gt; Phil?

00:00:20.624 --> 00:00:21.375
&gt;&gt; Pretty good.

00:00:21.375 --> 00:00:22.647
&gt;&gt; Welcome home.

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:23.617
&gt;&gt; It's great to be home.

00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:28.065
But I couldn't help but
be really pleased to see the dean

00:00:28.065 --> 00:00:31.955
wearing a business suit and
Nike shoes showing.

00:00:31.955 --> 00:00:34.523
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I think we can all be, and

00:00:34.523 --> 00:00:39.579
we can all be glad that basically I was
not the founder of Jockey underwear.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.904
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We'll try and

00:00:44.904 --> 00:00:47.880
make that a permanent change
in Dean Levin's wardrobe.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.732
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now Phil,

00:00:49.732 --> 00:00:52.773
we know you don't do too
many public speaking events.

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:57.154
And a question a lot of people have
asked is what does it take to get

00:00:57.154 --> 00:00:59.500
Phil Knight up on stage?

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.350
So I thought maybe we should share
the story of what got you here today.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:09.320
Now, you probably remember that we
first crossed paths a couple of months

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.890
ago just over there at McDonald Hall.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.756
You were entering the building when
I very politely interrupted you,

00:01:17.756 --> 00:01:19.249
introduced myself.

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:23.410
We exchanged a couple of pleasantries, and
then you went on inside about your day.

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.311
Now what you're probably less aware of,
Phil,

00:01:28.311 --> 00:01:33.921
is that I decide to camp outside
McDonald Hall for the next 40 minutes.

00:01:33.921 --> 00:01:37.599
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Watching every possible human exit to

00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:40.519
the building so that I could find you, and

00:01:40.519 --> 00:01:45.288
casually bump into you a second time,
to pitch you this interview.

00:01:45.288 --> 00:01:50.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's creepy, I know.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.554
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but that's how it happened.

00:01:53.554 --> 00:01:55.741
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll be honest,

00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:58.260
I was pretty nervous when you did
come out out that second time.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.886
It was fight or flight,
I very nearly walked the other way.

00:02:02.886 --> 00:02:05.487
But there was this voice in my head.

00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:08.845
It just kept saying, Will, just do it.

00:02:08.845 --> 00:02:12.633
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:18.696
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:18.696 --> 00:02:20.333
&gt;&gt; But really, Phil, we are so

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:23.610
thrilled and
excited to have you back on campus.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:27.885
And I think I speak for every student
here when I say how grateful we are for

00:02:27.885 --> 00:02:30.002
this incredible business school.

00:02:30.002 --> 00:02:35.252
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, but
having stalked me for at least 40 minutes.

00:02:35.252 --> 00:02:38.875
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I feel that there should be some

00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:39.696
payback.

00:02:39.696 --> 00:02:40.221
&gt;&gt; Don't do it.
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:40.221 --> 00:02:43.746
I know that most of you know
that Will's mother, Mags,

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:46.908
has come all the way from
Ireland to be here today.

00:02:46.908 --> 00:02:54.294
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And having come such a long way,

00:02:54.294 --> 00:02:58.609
I think you should reveal something
about yourself that she doesn't know.

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:03.688
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Phil,

00:03:03.688 --> 00:03:05.088
I don't know what you're
trying to suggest.

00:03:05.088 --> 00:03:06.113
I was a perfect child.

00:03:06.113 --> 00:03:07.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.109
did you make your bed this morning?

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:11.207
&gt;&gt; I didn't even sleep in my own bed this

00:03:11.207 --> 00:03:11.997
morning-
&gt;&gt; Well-

00:03:11.997 --> 00:03:12.983
&gt;&gt; because I gave it to my mom!

00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:15.072
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I gave it to my mom.

00:03:15.072 --> 00:03:18.617
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That wasn't what it sounded like.

00:03:18.617 --> 00:03:21.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right, now Phil.

00:03:21.805 --> 00:03:27.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Phil,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.560
we don't want to make you feel old,
this is payback by the way.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this could go back and
forth a long time, that's right.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.000
&gt;&gt; But it's been almost 60 years-
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.497
&gt;&gt; Since you were here in our shoes,
an incoming MBA, class of 1962.

00:03:45.497 --> 00:03:48.216
What kind of person was
Phil Knight back at the GSB?

00:03:48.216 --> 00:03:49.387
How would you have described yourself?

00:03:49.387 --> 00:03:50.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I will.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.670
But I want to tell you first
about coming back to campus,

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.820
after being gone for a while.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:04:00.880
That you go out 20 years or so, and
you haven't been back for a while, and

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.180
you come back, and it's more about just
sharing a moment with family and friends.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.270
It's for a moment,
you step back in history and

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:11.010
remember when you had all
the world laying out before you.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.829
And you decided the things you
were going to find important,

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:18.310
and it was really the start of you
becoming what you ultimately became.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.945
When I come back, for just a moment,

00:04:20.945 --> 00:04:26.650
I always hear the voice of Frank
Shallenberger, Bob Davis, Jim Porterfield.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:29.692
And I know that sounds sort of
corny to an educated group.

00:04:29.692 --> 00:04:33.144
But I promise you that over
half the people in this room,

00:04:33.144 --> 00:04:37.931
when they come back here 20 years from
now, will have those same feelings.

00:04:37.931 --> 00:04:43.545
For me it was, well, yeah,
that I was the guy that thought

00:04:43.545 --> 00:04:49.977
an extrovert was the person that
stared at other people's shoes.

00:04:49.977 --> 00:04:55.048
That it was a time for
me to kind of grow and

00:04:55.048 --> 00:04:59.981
chase what I was really going to become.

00:04:59.981 --> 00:05:03.709
And so it's really a big,
transitional period for me in my life.

00:05:03.709 --> 00:05:08.932
And as I say to this day, when I come
back, I still get inspired and lifted up.

00:05:08.932 --> 00:05:12.020
&gt;&gt; Do have any favorite memories
from your days back at Stanford?

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:18.700
&gt;&gt; That it was just a turning point in
general for me, that if I was honest,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:24.080
I'd say that my undergraduate
career was really focused on track.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.990
And it got to be,
when I got to be a graduate student,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:30.130
that I was really focused on
what it was I wanted to be.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.460
And this was just the right school at the
right time, with the right inspirational

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.360
professors, and it really doesn't
get any better than that.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.003
&gt;&gt; So you do graduate the GSB in 1962.

00:05:40.003 --> 00:05:40.757
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:44.279
&gt;&gt; This is a time when Silicon Valley
isn't even a phrase yet.

00:05:44.279 --> 00:05:44.980
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:48.080
&gt;&gt; And
you had what you call this crazy idea.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.640
What was the crazy idea and
where did it come about?

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:55.390
&gt;&gt; Well, I took the entrepreneurship
class from Frank Shallenberger.

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.700
And basically the project,
he had a term project

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:02.730
where you basically either attach
yourself to a company in the area, or for

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:07.150
the purpose of the paper, made up
a company and how it would succeed.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:12.290
And even in those days, a lot of my
classmates were writing about electronics.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.169
Which was certainly beyond me,
since when I turn on an electric switch,

00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:18.596
the light bulb comes on,
it's magic for me.

00:06:18.596 --> 00:06:20.086
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:06:20.086 --> 00:06:25.027
my old track coach was always working with
shoes, and felt that a light weight in

00:06:25.027 --> 00:06:31.190
shoes was something that was neglected by
the major manufacturers, Adidas and Puma.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:35.820
And indeed, my senior year, Otis Davis won
the Pacific Coast Conference championship

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.500
in a pair of homemade Bowerman shoes,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.020
which were an ounce lighter
than the other shoes.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.980
So I kind of put those things together,
and

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.470
said if you were starting a shoe factory,
would you start it in Germany?

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.740
And I said it's such
a labor intensive business,

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.970
it makes more sense to start it in Japan.

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:56.428
Japan being a country that took German
cameras, and dominated the camera markets,

00:06:56.428 --> 00:06:58.158
I said, could they do the same
thing in sport shoes?

00:06:58.158 --> 00:07:02.140
And that was really the thesis of the
paper which ultimately I got caught up in,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.930
and here we are.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:05.160
&gt;&gt; Now, you got an A on that paper, right?

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:05.957
&gt;&gt; I did.

00:07:05.957 --> 00:07:08.785
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That mattered.

00:07:08.785 --> 00:07:11.776
If he had ridiculed the paper,
I don't know where we'd be today.

00:07:11.776 --> 00:07:14.024
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:07:14.024 --> 00:07:18.410
Phil, this was a time when
entrepreneurship post-GSB was not just

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.340
a less traveled path,
it was probably a less celebrated path.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.850
What gave you the confidence
to push ahead?

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:28.943
&gt;&gt; Well, if I didn't,
I was going to have to be an accountant.

00:07:28.943 --> 00:07:32.013
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I know that's a scary thought.

00:07:32.013 --> 00:07:34.330
&gt;&gt; No, it was-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:37.730
&gt;&gt; No, but it was when I wrote the paper,

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.800
it just stayed in my head.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:47.280
And at the time, I think the ratio was
that 26 out of 27 new companies failed.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.860
So I knew the odds weren't high.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:53.440
But I really began to feel if I could do
this, that it would really be meaningful

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.390
to me, and something that I would really
have a lot of passion for and about.

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:01.955
And so that's where it started and
the way it will end.

00:08:01.955 --> 00:08:03.200
&gt;&gt; Now, two years in

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:08.350
Many of your classmates have gone
down more traditional career paths.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:13.210
You're back in Oregon, you're living
in your old room in the family house,

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:16.120
and you're selling shoes out
of the trunk of your car.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.986
Did you ever have doubts in those moments,
and what was it that kept you going?

00:08:22.766 --> 00:08:27.643
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well obviously,
there were a lot of doubts, but

00:08:27.643 --> 00:08:30.400
I enjoyed what I was doing, and

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.491
I really thought that I was
bringing a product to the world

00:08:35.491 --> 00:08:40.600
that was better than the other product and
so I believed.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.490
And if you read the book,

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:47.000
it wasn't too long before I had a bunch
of other people that believed as well.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.520
And we just through all the ups and

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:54.110
downs and there were plenty of downs
that we never stopped believing.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.150
And a lot of people say,
why did you stay with it?

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.170
There were a lot of negative moments and
a lot of downturns.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:05.900
And I look back on those days
the most fun I ever had in business.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.150
Every Monday you went to work and
you knew it mattered what you did,

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.750
including whether you're
going to meet payroll on Friday.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:19.960
But it was an exciting time
that Frank Shellenberger

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.938
had done a wonderful job of preparing
us for being an entrepreneur.

00:09:23.938 --> 00:09:27.630
Bob Davis, a marketing professor said,
if you're going to be an entrepreneur,

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.755
he said, everyday's a crisis and
every Friday is a Jesus crisis.

00:09:30.755 --> 00:09:33.995
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that was kind of the way it was.

00:09:33.995 --> 00:09:36.799
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now you also talk in your book,

00:09:36.799 --> 00:09:39.266
Shoe Dog, about those early trips to Asia.

00:09:39.266 --> 00:09:40.300
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:47.690
&gt;&gt; So you're 24 years old, traveling alone
to Japan, it's a culture you don't know,

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:53.280
a language you don't speak, to try and
broker that first shoe import deal.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:59.160
So this was a time before Stanford's
global study trips or GMIXs.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.989
Where did that ability to embrace hustle
and uncertainty come from in you?

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:09.792
&gt;&gt; Well,
by the time I got there I believed, and so

00:10:09.792 --> 00:10:14.854
I believed that that was a good idea,
I believed that

00:10:14.854 --> 00:10:19.574
if we could get a cooperative
Japanese factory,

00:10:19.574 --> 00:10:26.720
that we could make the idea work,
and so I overcame, I'm an introvert.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:30.920
Well, you're basically you have to
believe, you have to overcome the shyness

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:35.035
to take certain chances and
I was willing to do that, and

00:10:35.035 --> 00:10:40.036
there were a lot of ups and
downs, but ultimately it worked.

00:10:40.036 --> 00:10:42.560
And I think at the end of
the day you gotta believe.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.268
And that's what I did have
maybe above everything else.

00:10:46.268 --> 00:10:50.191
&gt;&gt; Now as you said, the team grew and
you weren't alone in this endeavor.

00:10:50.191 --> 00:10:54.735
And I think there's no story of
Nike without, of course, mentioning

00:10:54.735 --> 00:11:00.480
Bill Bowerman, your former track coach at
Oregon, later US Olympic's track coach.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.600
And of course,
your co-founder of what became Nike.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.625
You've described yourself as
a person whose always needed heroes.

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:08.930
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:13.150
&gt;&gt; Why was Bill a hero to you and
what is it you learned from him?

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:18.780
&gt;&gt; Well he was a very unique person
that he was probably the best track and

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.800
field coach in the world, for one thing.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.012
But he also had a certain command
presence and a certain belief and

00:11:26.012 --> 00:11:29.720
certain leadership
things that leaders did.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.780
He said, I'm not a track coach, he says,
I'm a professor of competitive response.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:42.590
And he had a way of having sayings
that got your attention and

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.590
really focused you on attitude.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:46.890
The strategy for
running the mile was start out and

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.520
run the first two laps
at a very fast pace.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.925
Run the third lap as fast as you can,
run the fourth lap, triple your speed.

00:11:53.925 --> 00:12:00.809
And he said, the Oregon Trail,
he says, the cowards never started and

00:12:00.809 --> 00:12:05.878
the weak died along the way,
that leaves you and me.

00:12:05.878 --> 00:12:10.495
And that's once again,
him getting in kind of your attitude and

00:12:10.495 --> 00:12:14.524
his best one for me was always,
do right and fear no man.

00:12:14.524 --> 00:12:16.709
And so he had a big influence on my life,

00:12:16.709 --> 00:12:19.902
certainly the biggest influence
other than my parents.

00:12:19.902 --> 00:12:22.630
And if you go back through the sayings and
who he was,

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.494
he was always working on attitude.

00:12:24.494 --> 00:12:29.824
This is a man that had seen combat
in World War II, fired a gun and

00:12:29.824 --> 00:12:36.480
been fired at, and he had seen
competition at its most extreme level.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.380
And he thought his job

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:44.400
as a professor of competitive response
was to get kids ready, young men and

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.360
young women, ultimately to be ready for
the toughest competition they can face.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.600
So he brought, to me, a certain attitude,

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.650
which you can be an introvert and
have attitude, so that was sort of me.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:00.320
And he had a huge influence on that,

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.430
and he was a hero of mine,
in those days, and unto this day.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:09.340
&gt;&gt; When you first met him, you described
it as love and fear at first sight.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:12.932
&gt;&gt; Right, and then there's little
hate that came after that.

00:13:12.932 --> 00:13:16.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He really kind of believed in hazing,

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:19.866
that you had to prove that
you wanted to be there.

00:13:19.866 --> 00:13:27.015
And so he would make you go through hoops,
which I did, somewhat reluctantly.

00:13:27.015 --> 00:13:28.993
&gt;&gt; Now beyond Bill of course,

00:13:28.993 --> 00:13:33.880
you talk with a lot of affection
about that core early Nike team.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.354
And I think you had a name for
each other, right?

00:13:36.354 --> 00:13:42.111
&gt;&gt; Well yeah, we did.

00:13:42.111 --> 00:13:44.275
&gt;&gt; I see you're playing that game.

00:13:44.275 --> 00:13:48.424
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Now, the first four or
five people of the company,

00:13:48.424 --> 00:13:54.230
we wound up calling each other butt
faces which, we don't do that anymore.

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:54.755
We're
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:54.755 --> 00:13:56.074
&gt;&gt; More professional group now.

00:13:56.074 --> 00:13:57.619
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You know from the top team,

00:13:57.619 --> 00:13:59.583
reached a similar name for
each other actually.

00:13:59.583 --> 00:14:02.284
&gt;&gt; It's good, that's healthy.

00:14:02.284 --> 00:14:06.670
&gt;&gt; But what was it about that early
team and that culture that made it so

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:07.710
successful?

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:13.090
&gt;&gt; Well I think first of all, they
were a bizarre group to say the least.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:15.851
Each of them, in his own way,
but they were,

00:14:15.851 --> 00:14:19.268
and I hope this showed in the book,
that they were able,

00:14:19.268 --> 00:14:24.183
very able people that really kind of
didn't get along with society in general.

00:14:24.183 --> 00:14:25.896
So it was kind of a perfect fit.

00:14:25.896 --> 00:14:29.150
And they complimented each other
in terms of their abilities.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:32.900
And then lastly, all of them believed.

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:36.150
When we would hit a downturn, nobody
thought, this is the end, versus, say,

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:38.680
okay, we've got to get together and
find our way out of this one.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:42.070
And so it was fun being in
a foxhole with those guys.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.900
And basically, it's corny,
but we loved each other, and

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.270
we were going to go through
this come hell or high water.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:56.100
And It was a very exciting intense time
and it, it's actually, I love those times.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:57.960
&gt;&gt; You fought a lot of crisis together.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:14:59.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for sure.

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.460
&gt;&gt; In recent years Phil,

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:06.100
we've seen many high profile disruptive
companies being pressured to rein in.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:11.010
What we're seeing is aggressive,
macho, win at all costs cultures,

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.630
cultures that have brought these companies
so much early success in their life.

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:20.590
Just last year,
Nike faced some similar accusations and

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.280
the management team made some
pretty decisive changes.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:30.330
When you grow from a start up to a 74,000
person team, how do you preserve

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:35.770
the best of that winning culture in a more
complex, diverse, global workforce?

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:37.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
well you touched on a lot of things there,

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:44.910
I do think that Nike's culture is
a big part of its reason for success.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:49.710
And the culture really was formed in
the early days by the four people,

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:55.020
plus me, and it still exists to this day.

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:58.700
A lot of people say, well, Phil had a
dominant influence on that, and he didn't.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:02.320
I've often said many times, Nike is
young and irreverent, and I'm neither.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:09.890
But it did, to use Burman's word,
it came with a certain attitude.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:12.180
And it was sort of us.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.670
It's who we were.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:17.590
And the culture has been modified some,
which is a good thing.

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:19.530
But it's still, kind of,
basically there, and

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:21.130
I thinks its a big part of it's strength.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:32.370
Yeah, so I hope that that culture is
basically in place 20 years from now.

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:36.635
And it's one thing it fits
with sort of our view then and

00:16:36.635 --> 00:16:38.684
now about what a brand is.

00:16:38.684 --> 00:16:42.073
It doesn't matter how many
people hate your brand,

00:16:42.073 --> 00:16:44.238
as long as enough people love it.

00:16:44.238 --> 00:16:49.670
And as long as you have that out,
you can't be afraid of offending people.

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:51.760
You can't try and
go down the middle of the road.

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:54.750
You have to take a stand on something,
which is ultimately,

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.280
I think why the Kaepernick ad worked.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.230
&gt;&gt; Well let's jump into those athletes.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.860
So, I want to take you back to 1978, Phil.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.456
You stumble across a shy, timid,

00:17:05.456 --> 00:17:09.835
retiring Stanford tennis player
by the name of John McEnroe.

00:17:09.835 --> 00:17:11.157
&gt;&gt; Timid, I wouldn't say.

00:17:11.157 --> 00:17:14.200
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.660
we got to see a little bit
of John in that video.

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:21.490
But for anyone who doesn't know tennis
John is a young, fantastic tennis

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:26.190
player who's as well known for
breaking rackets as he is breaking serve.

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:30.400
He is everything that the tennis
establishment at the time,

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:33.230
the major majority of the market, was not.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:36.870
So what made him the right ambassador for
Nike?

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:42.050
&gt;&gt; Well the story, basically, we

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:50.480
had an agreement with Jimmy Connors'
agent for him to wear Nike shoes.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:54.130
And he did, he wore them at Wimbledon.

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:58.395
And the agent said, as soon as he gets
back from Wimbledon, we'll get it signed.

00:17:58.395 --> 00:17:59.633
Well, he won Wimbledon.

00:17:59.633 --> 00:18:02.834
And as soon as he got back from Europe for
he had summer matches and

00:18:02.834 --> 00:18:03.976
then came the US Open.

00:18:03.976 --> 00:18:06.857
He says, as we get done with the US Open,
we'll get this contract signed.

00:18:06.857 --> 00:18:10.740
Well, he won the US Open, and then,
we got down to sign the contract.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:11.606
And he says, I don't remember it this way.

00:18:11.606 --> 00:18:14.603
That was Jimmy Connors' agent,
not Jimmy Connors, himself.

00:18:14.603 --> 00:18:15.569
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:18:15.569 --> 00:18:17.940
that was the end of our
endorsement with Jimmy Connors.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:21.630
[LAUGH] And so, the next year,
I think it was,

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:25.990
that I went to Wimbledon looking for
the next great hope.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:30.234
And there were a bunch of really good
American, young Americans 18 and under.

00:18:30.234 --> 00:18:32.030
I was looking at the 18 and unders.

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.567
And they had Brian Godfrey
who was a great player and

00:18:34.567 --> 00:18:36.396
Eliot Teltscher is a great player and

00:18:36.396 --> 00:18:40.185
the head of USA tennis was saying that
those are really great endorsements.

00:18:40.185 --> 00:18:42.692
Stay away from that kid McEnroe
he's too much of a hot head.

00:18:42.692 --> 00:18:45.821
He's playing over on court 14 and
I went right for court 14.

00:18:45.821 --> 00:18:50.152
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And basically that week he was playing

00:18:50.152 --> 00:18:55.300
the number 16 player in the world,
Phil Dent, and he's 18 years old and

00:18:55.300 --> 00:19:00.220
hits his first serve in and the chalk
flies up and the line judge goes out.

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:04.550
McEnroe jumps over the net, puts his
nose this far from the official and

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.440
says are you sure of that call?

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.050
Are you very sure of that call?

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:11.200
And I turned to the guy I was with and
said this kid's not afraid.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.600
And I kind of became a fan
of his intensity and

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:17.825
his competitiveness and his attitude.

00:19:17.825 --> 00:19:21.080
He did lose control sometimes.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:24.064
But-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:19:24.064 --> 00:19:26.375
&gt;&gt; Well, I always remember Frank DeForge's

00:19:26.375 --> 00:19:30.673
write-up of him in Sports Illustrated,
when he said of the big picture of Mac,

00:19:30.673 --> 00:19:33.400
and it says, why isn't this man smiling?

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:35.268
Said, Beethoven didn't smile much, either.

00:19:35.268 --> 00:19:38.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's fair.

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:42.090
A few years later, actually, several years
later, you decided to name the executive

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:45.920
building at your headquarters
in Oregon after John McEnroe.

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:48.970
What is it you were trying to say
to the team at Nike in doing that?

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:55.500
&gt;&gt; Well we named all the buildings
after our heroes at the time, and

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:59.550
he was one of them, and
that just that was where my office was.

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:01.310
&gt;&gt; Fair enough.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:02.930
You're glad to see him back, playing well?

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:04.940
&gt;&gt; And that makes me feel even better.

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.467
&gt;&gt; Now, let's fast forward to 1984.

00:20:06.467 --> 00:20:11.755
You come across another decent basketball
player whose name is Michael Jordan?

00:20:11.755 --> 00:20:13.413
Michael Jordan, that's it.

00:20:13.413 --> 00:20:15.423
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:20:15.423 --> 00:20:19.335
Nike were actually criticized at the time
for overpaying for Jordan's signature.

00:20:19.335 --> 00:20:19.930
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.320
&gt;&gt; But of course, with hindsight,
he became more than an athlete,

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.590
he became a $2.5 billion revenue brand for
Nike.

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:33.200
Beyond MJ's greatness,
what was it that made that brand so

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:34.910
successful, and so dominant?

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
it isn't something you can bottle.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:42.425
It's been truly one of
the unique experiences really,

00:20:42.425 --> 00:20:47.560
in all of marketing,
that obviously he had it all.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:49.600
And we pretty much knew that coming in.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:53.207
I mean, he was player of the year,
he was handsome, he could jump, and

00:20:53.207 --> 00:20:56.351
that he won the national
championship with the winning shot.

00:20:56.351 --> 00:20:59.698
He had all of it, and when we signed him,

00:20:59.698 --> 00:21:04.627
we signed him for
more than any rookie had been paid before.

00:21:04.627 --> 00:21:07.907
We signed him for $250,000 a year, and

00:21:07.907 --> 00:21:11.930
Fortune magazine ran a little
insert in another article.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:15.620
It says there's no greater indication that
Nike has lost its way than the fact that

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:18.908
they paid Michael Jordan $250,000.

00:21:18.908 --> 00:21:23.344
But we combined it with
what we thought was

00:21:23.344 --> 00:21:28.556
a really good shoe,
a really distinctive shoe.

00:21:28.556 --> 00:21:31.830
It was red and
black it wasn't just white or black.

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:35.600
And of course he wore it
in great performances.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:39.115
And then we had the added benefit of
David Stern banned it in the NBA.

00:21:39.115 --> 00:21:42.797
So we immediately combined
what became a good ad,

00:21:42.797 --> 00:21:47.629
said, banned in the NBA, and
every kid in the world wanted that.

00:21:47.629 --> 00:21:49.602
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it hasn't been straight uphill.

00:21:49.602 --> 00:21:52.440
We had a one year where our
sales actually went down.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:57.230
But overall, we got together
with really good advertising,

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:00.580
which reflected his personality,
which was strong.

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:06.611
And it's been an unbelievable
success that when he quit playing,

00:22:06.611 --> 00:22:11.576
sales of Jordan brand
product were $750 million.

00:22:11.576 --> 00:22:16.717
And this last fiscal year, they topped
$3 billion long after he quit playing.

00:22:16.717 --> 00:22:21.759
So I said to many people imagine how much
we would sell if he never played [LAUGH]

00:22:21.759 --> 00:22:23.809
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:23.809 --> 00:22:24.665
&gt;&gt; We can only dream.

00:22:24.665 --> 00:22:29.400
&gt;&gt; Phil, it's Nike's 55th
anniversary next week, I believe.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.477
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; The company's at record revenue levels,

00:22:32.477 --> 00:22:35.411
and just last year Nike
was voted alongside

00:22:35.411 --> 00:22:38.443
Apple as the favorite brand of millenials.

00:22:38.443 --> 00:22:41.664
And I think there's a few of us
in here who might attest to that.

00:22:41.664 --> 00:22:48.760
How does a brand stay so relevant to so
many people for so long?

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:53.320
&gt;&gt; Well, it's hard work, but
you have to work at it all the time.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:56.970
But I think our thing obviously
it starts with the product,

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:01.870
the product is by far our most
important marketing tool.

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:04.480
And so we're constantly
working to improve product.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:05.440
And so

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:10.770
the kind of the recent up turn is because
we've had a really good product pipeline.

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:15.510
Probably, the React running shoe
is the number one on the list,

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:19.670
but there have been a lot of others and
so that's been really important.

00:23:19.670 --> 00:23:23.950
And then good advertising
is critical to it.

00:23:25.152 --> 00:23:29.990
The Wieden+Kennedy experience has been,
is in many

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:34.070
ways as dramatic as Nike's, and it's
really been kind of along side each other.

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:38.550
When I first met Dan Wieden, I walked
into the door of his quote office.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:42.870
He had three people besides himself
sitting around the card table.

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:44.847
And he said welcome to Wieden+Kennedy and
I said, Dan,

00:23:44.847 --> 00:23:47.980
just I want you to know just one thing,
I hate advertising.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:50.380
And he looked at me and said,
well this will be interesting.

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:51.332
And it has been.

00:23:51.332 --> 00:23:56.932
But it is interesting that obviously
if I had said that to Procter &amp; Gamble,

00:23:56.932 --> 00:24:00.520
they'd take your $25
advertising budget and

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:05.412
go on out the door But
they were a small advertising firm, and

00:24:05.412 --> 00:24:10.935
he grew to understand that what I didn't
mean was that I hated advertising,

00:24:10.935 --> 00:24:16.717
what I meant was that I hate traditional
advertising and he found ways to find out

00:24:16.717 --> 00:24:22.013
who Nike is, or who Michael Jordan was and
project that to the consumer.

00:24:22.013 --> 00:24:24.964
And not only did Nike succeed,

00:24:24.964 --> 00:24:30.518
Kennedy now has offices all over
the world, over 1,000 employees.

00:24:30.518 --> 00:24:33.443
It has Coca Cola as a client,
it has Honda as a client,

00:24:33.443 --> 00:24:36.630
it has a lot of the world's
most famous brands as clients.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:42.810
They've been an enormous success, and to
this day they work really hard at trying

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:48.870
to find out who it is and who the athlete
is and how to how to project them.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:52.740
I know we had Boris Becker,
the great tennis players, and

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:54.200
we're trying to get him as an endorsement.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:58.443
And Dan Widen, was making the pitch and
he says, we will find out who you are and

00:24:58.443 --> 00:24:59.938
project that to the world.

00:24:59.938 --> 00:25:05.753
And Boris says, how in the world can you
know who I am, when I don't know who I am?

00:25:05.753 --> 00:25:08.030
[LAUGH] And just says,
well, we'll find out.

00:25:08.030 --> 00:25:10.310
Well, we didn't make the sale,

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:14.630
we didn't sell them, but
that is one of the things I've done.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:18.773
So the advertising and the product are two
of the really important things, and

00:25:18.773 --> 00:25:22.180
then keeping the product fresh and
keeping advertising fresh.

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:25.506
And it's an ongoing challenge and
we don't always hit it.

00:25:25.506 --> 00:25:28.609
A couple years ago we took
a little dip and it's a very,

00:25:28.609 --> 00:25:32.995
very competitive business and every six
months there's kind of a new life and

00:25:32.995 --> 00:25:34.970
everybody has to be aware of that.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:40.320
&gt;&gt; Phil this is a school and
a speaker series dedicated to leadership.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:43.640
So we want to make sure
we touch on that topic.

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:48.514
Something the MBA I class is challenged
with each year is why would someone

00:25:48.514 --> 00:25:49.388
follow you?

00:25:49.388 --> 00:25:52.978
I think, there can be a stereotype
of what a CEO should be.

00:25:52.978 --> 00:25:56.458
Sometimes, we think of them as
a natural extrovert around the valley,

00:25:56.458 --> 00:25:58.782
sometimes they can be
hero-like characters.

00:25:58.782 --> 00:26:02.890
You described yourself as shy,
introverted,

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:06.799
someone who identifies
with the born loser.

00:26:06.799 --> 00:26:09.751
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's why [CROSSTALK]

00:26:09.751 --> 00:26:11.735
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:11.735 --> 00:26:13.836
&gt;&gt; But how have those traits helped you as

00:26:13.836 --> 00:26:14.910
you built Nike?

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:19.833
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, I mean obviously
introverted people have tendency to listen

00:26:19.833 --> 00:26:21.813
which I think good leaders do.

00:26:21.813 --> 00:26:27.340
But when I was in school that, they talked
about two different types of leadership.

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:31.020
They talked about autocratic
leadership and democratic leadership.

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:36.250
And it was only 15 years after
World War II and they had

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:41.500
two of the great autocratic leaders ever,
Douglas MacArthur and George Patton.

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:45.334
And they at the time were looked
on as being very successful.

00:26:45.334 --> 00:26:50.364
That then you have a democratic
style leadership which is,

00:26:50.364 --> 00:26:56.009
the other thing is getting people
involved and talking to people.

00:26:56.009 --> 00:27:00.121
So I think it's probably
almost impossible for

00:27:00.121 --> 00:27:03.731
an introvert to be an autocratic leader.

00:27:03.731 --> 00:27:07.814
But an introvert can be a democratic
leader and as the idea of democratic

00:27:07.814 --> 00:27:12.578
leadership progressed, they started to
use the term collaborative leadership,

00:27:12.578 --> 00:27:17.750
which is really, in my view, the ultimate
only kind of leadership to this day.

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:20.666
That in this day and age,
the autocratic leader doesn't work.

00:27:20.666 --> 00:27:24.969
It can work for temporarily but
it won't work long run.

00:27:24.969 --> 00:27:29.725
And I hope that the people in this
auditorium today don't get the idea

00:27:29.725 --> 00:27:33.010
that hero leadership
means being autocratic.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:39.920
I mean, obviously, I think Steve Jobs is
looked at as really kind of in this area,

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:44.180
the whole world really as being one of
the great leaders, which I believe he was.

00:27:44.180 --> 00:27:49.070
But I do believe at the end of his career,
he was beginning to modify his natural

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:54.490
autocratic style which if you will, got
him fired as first tour around Apple and

00:27:54.490 --> 00:27:59.470
as he got it to go through Apple a second
time, there was a change in Steve Jobs.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:05.060
And you notice that from the commencement
speech he gave to Stanford University and

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:08.560
he hired Tim Cook and he had a lot
of give and take with Tim Cook.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:13.450
And Tim Cook who I know quite well is
a very much collaborative leader, and

00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:14.900
I believe a great collaborative leader.

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:19.422
So I think that it's just don't
equate hero leadership with

00:28:19.422 --> 00:28:21.382
autocratic leadership.

00:28:21.382 --> 00:28:25.152
The only task style of leadership
that will work in this day and

00:28:25.152 --> 00:28:27.340
age is collaborative leadership.

00:28:28.622 --> 00:28:31.600
&gt;&gt; Okay, Phil, another thing they
know about this group is that we're

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:33.660
a pretty touchy-feely bunch these days.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:38.440
Of course, in your book you describe
business as war without bullets.

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:39.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:43.996
&gt;&gt; So, as you look back on those early
days in Nike dealing with those crises,

00:28:43.996 --> 00:28:49.460
were soft skills tools an entrepreneur
like you could really afford back then?.

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:52.447
&gt;&gt; What type of skills
are you talking about?

00:28:52.447 --> 00:28:54.400
&gt;&gt; The softer skills,
the touchy-feely skills.

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:59.316
&gt;&gt; I think yeah, I mean,
yeah, I think that.

00:29:02.468 --> 00:29:06.927
If you read the book Shoe Dog,
obviously, I don't come across as

00:29:06.927 --> 00:29:11.380
a touchy-feely type, and
neither were any of those top five.

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:14.300
But there's lots of different
ways of communicating.

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:19.190
And one of the things I don't like
about the politically correct movement

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:24.060
is that to me, politically correct
has been founded by people that

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:27.690
couldn't communicate, and they don't want
you to communicate very well either.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:31.515
Because communication is really,
is a kind of an individual thing.

00:29:31.515 --> 00:29:32.970
Everybody's a little bit different.

00:29:32.970 --> 00:29:35.238
It's like people have
different fingerprints,

00:29:35.238 --> 00:29:38.430
they have different personalities,
they have different emotions.

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:41.297
And I think the leader's job
is to know who his team is and

00:29:41.297 --> 00:29:44.174
there is different ways to
communicate with that team.

00:29:44.174 --> 00:29:48.080
We weren't touchy-feely in the sense that
we are always patting each other back and

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:48.859
say how we feel.

00:29:48.859 --> 00:29:51.455
But you could say, how are you
doing today, you son of a bitch and

00:29:51.455 --> 00:29:52.355
mean the same thing.

00:29:52.355 --> 00:29:56.475
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so there's just

00:29:56.475 --> 00:30:00.885
different ways to do that.

00:30:00.885 --> 00:30:04.125
&gt;&gt; I think we need to bring that into
the chatter on campus these days.

00:30:04.125 --> 00:30:09.439
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, Phil, we also study a lot here,

00:30:09.439 --> 00:30:15.680
how leaders act in times of crisis, and
Nike and yourself have dealt with several.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:19.610
Today the Nike brand is stronger than
ever but of course in the late 90s,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:25.160
Nike did for some time become synonymous
with sweat shops, and slave wages.

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:29.950
As you look back how do you
feel you handled that crisis,

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:32.318
what would you do differently,
and what are you most proud of?

00:30:32.318 --> 00:30:39.490
&gt;&gt; Well, one of the things

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:44.570
that we didn't talk about in business
school when I was here was the media.

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:50.240
And so I think probably this group Is
probably much more aware of the media.

00:30:50.240 --> 00:30:54.970
But it's always there, it's more
prevalent than it's ever been and

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:57.200
they're not always consumed by facts.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:03.230
That and so
when the first charges came that

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:08.383
Nike runs sweatshops

00:31:08.383 --> 00:31:14.130
that it's really a seductive
argument to say,

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:20.180
this person only makes $4 a day, and
Michael Jordan makes $30 million a year,

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:24.730
and Phil Knight's worth $2 million
dollars, and isn't this awful.

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:31.370
But our initial reaction was
that we don't run sweatshops.

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:35.643
And that you're wrong.

00:31:35.643 --> 00:31:41.500
And I was the CEO at the time, and
that was my strategy, and it was awful.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:42.876
It didn't work.

00:31:42.876 --> 00:31:48.156
And so we kind of after about
a year of that it became clear.

00:31:48.156 --> 00:31:51.953
And so we took another tack which says
yeah you can criticize who you want but

00:31:51.953 --> 00:31:56.048
this is what we're going to be and this is
how were going to be a better company over

00:31:56.048 --> 00:31:59.482
the next 10 years and we're going
to take these steps each year and

00:31:59.482 --> 00:32:03.920
you come look at any of our factories
anywhere in the world that you want.

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.580
And we've kind of been on that path
ever since and that's basically working.

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:10.320
And I do think that

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:14.540
we have had lots of good comments that
don't get much publicity from the media.

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:19.080
And one of the good ones was a member of
the United Nations says Nike's the gold

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:23.190
standard for all apparel companies
in how they run their factories.

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:27.930
And it's a constant battle though,
because we're always kind of looking for

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:29.720
new factories or changing factories.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:33.010
And most of our shoe factories and

00:32:33.010 --> 00:32:36.680
our good apparel factories have been
with us for 20 or 25 years, but

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:40.170
economics change at different times so you
do change some of them on the periphery.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:43.370
So it's a constant battle to live
up to the standards we have.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:47.580
But we have a whole team at Nike
that does that all the time and

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:51.010
basically is talking to people on
the production line all the time.

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:55.510
And, I do think that our factories
are the best in the world right now for

00:32:55.510 --> 00:33:01.628
conditions and it's a basic truth that
great shoes are made by great factories.

00:33:01.628 --> 00:33:07.867
You don't want a bad factory making
the best shoes in the world.

00:33:07.867 --> 00:33:11.390
&gt;&gt; Now Phil, something else we
hear a lot from the media but

00:33:11.390 --> 00:33:13.970
also occasionally at
Stanford is to find and

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:19.790
follow your passion and
you are perhaps the example of someone who

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:23.895
built a business dedicated to something
they love, which is sports and athletes.

00:33:23.895 --> 00:33:28.030
There's many of us in the room today
who I feel they are struggling

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:32.950
to maybe tie our true passions
to a viable business or career.

00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:35.800
What would you say to people like us?

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:39.060
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, you know, that one
thing that is left out is that you've

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:42.150
got to have a niche,
you've got to have a reason to succeed.

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:48.411
My reason that was that, you know,
Japan could make shoes economically, and

00:33:48.411 --> 00:33:54.370
but then the rest of it fit
right in home base for me.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:58.790
So and I do think if you're going to be an
entrepreneur it has to be something that

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:02.170
you really love because there
will be a lot of dark days.

00:34:02.170 --> 00:34:06.610
And as I say we never hesitated
in those dark days so

00:34:06.610 --> 00:34:11.270
I was fortunate enough to find what I
thought was a niche to go with my passion,

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:14.070
and that's what you need to
bring those two things together.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:17.060
But when you do, you're very,
very fortunate and take advantage of it.

00:34:18.160 --> 00:34:20.950
&gt;&gt; All right, well, look, Phil,
this has been brilliant thus far.

00:34:20.950 --> 00:34:23.170
I know Nike say there is no finish line.

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:24.960
We do need to finish in about 15 minutes.

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:26.280
Phil, this has been brilliant.

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:29.900
We're almost going to let you go,
not quite yet.

00:34:29.900 --> 00:34:32.210
There's a little tradition we
like to do up here, on stage,

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:33.440
we call the lightning round.

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:37.220
So the idea is, I'm going to ask
you a set of either-or questions.

00:34:37.220 --> 00:34:41.335
We want you to answer the one answer
that comes first in your mind, okay?

00:34:41.335 --> 00:34:45.035
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You didn't warn me about this.

00:34:45.035 --> 00:34:46.417
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You didn't tell me you were going to

00:34:46.417 --> 00:34:47.020
mention me mom.

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:48.124
&gt;&gt; I might get back to her.

00:34:48.124 --> 00:34:49.701
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:49.701 --> 00:34:51.616
&gt;&gt; All right, look.

00:34:51.616 --> 00:34:54.300
Because it's a new thing
we're going to start easy and

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:55.952
then we'll get harder, okay?

00:34:55.952 --> 00:34:58.963
So Jordan or LeBron?

00:34:58.963 --> 00:35:01.454
&gt;&gt; No.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]

00:35:01.454 --> 00:35:05.397
&gt;&gt; It's like asking which of your kids do

00:35:05.397 --> 00:35:07.252
you like better.

00:35:07.252 --> 00:35:10.705
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You can tell me backstage it's fine.

00:35:10.705 --> 00:35:12.513
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Rose Bowl or Super Bowl?

00:35:12.513 --> 00:35:14.604
&gt;&gt; Rose Bowl.

00:35:14.604 --> 00:35:16.997
&gt;&gt; 3:40 mile or two-hour marathon?

00:35:20.868 --> 00:35:22.520
&gt;&gt; Two hour marathon.

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:24.688
&gt;&gt; Nice, suit or track suit?

00:35:24.688 --> 00:35:26.914
&gt;&gt; Man, track suit.

00:35:26.914 --> 00:35:30.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Just do it, or dream crazy?

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:31.070
&gt;&gt; Just do it.

00:35:32.180 --> 00:35:34.080
&gt;&gt; Hawaii or Palm Springs?

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:34.580
&gt;&gt; Hawaii.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:38.070
&gt;&gt; Beatles or Rolling Stones?

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:39.411
&gt;&gt; Beatles.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:39.411 --> 00:35:40.551
&gt;&gt; That was easy.

00:35:40.551 --> 00:35:42.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Breaking Bad or The Sopranos?

00:35:45.230 --> 00:35:46.044
&gt;&gt; Ooh.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:35:46.044 --> 00:35:48.647
&gt;&gt; Probably Sopranos but that's tough.

00:35:48.647 --> 00:35:50.513
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We were told it was tough for you.

00:35:50.513 --> 00:35:53.416
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Adidas or barefoot?

00:35:53.416 --> 00:35:54.766
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Barefoot.

00:35:54.766 --> 00:36:00.101
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen, Phil Knight.

00:36:00.101 --> 00:36:07.273
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thanks, it was fun.

00:36:07.273 --> 00:36:13.068
[MUSIC]

